---
<BaseLayout title="Historical economics of whaling" description="Why commercial whaling developed: products, markets, livelihoods and changing industries, with scoped economic examples and original sources.">
  <header class="container history-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">History & economics</p>
    <h1>Products, markets,<br /> and the people behind them.</h1>
    <p>Whaling supplied food, lighting materials, lubricants and manufactured goods. Its economic purposes changed across places and periods. Understanding those differences is part of documenting the history.</p>
    <p class="history-date">Research edition · October 20, 2026 · Based on the October 9 research archive</p>
    <a class="text-link" href="/countries/history/">Explore the country-history inventory →</a>
  </header>
  <div class="container history-layout">
    <nav class="history-contents" aria-label="On this page"><p class="eyebrow">On this page</p><a href="#products">What was sold</a><a href="#values">What the figures mean</a><a href="#change">How industries changed</a><a href="#communities">People and livelihoods</a><a href="#method">Evidence and open questions</a></nav>
    <article class="history-prose">
      <section id="products"><h2>More than a single product.</h2>
        <p>New Bedford Whaling Museum distinguishes ordinary whale oil, sperm oil, spermaceti and baleen. Oils served lighting and machinery; refined spermaceti supplied candles; baleen provided flexible support for clothing and other goods. These were different materials with different markets.</p>
        <p>Later demand also mattered. A Dutch museum account connects postwar whaling to edible fats and margarine. Brazil’s 1982 SUDEPE report describes meat exports and domestic food markets, alongside oil for industrial uses and meal for agriculture and animal feed.</p>
        <p>A documented use does not prove that a material was universally indispensable. Cost, performance, access and preference must be established for the particular place and period.</p>
        <HistorySources ids={['S007','S042','S003']} />
      </section>
      <section id="values"><h2>Economic evidence, with its units attached.</h2>
        <p>Sales, profit, landed value and compensation measure different things. These examples show what the surviving records can tell us; they are not a country ranking or a world total.</p>
        <div class="economic-observations">{observations.map(item => <section><h3>{item.place}</h3><p class="economic-value">{item.value}</p><p>{item.scope}</p><a href={item.href}>Read the source ({item.source}) →</a></section>)}</div>
        <p>No new inflation adjustment or exchange-rate conversion has been applied. Landed and processed values must not be added as if they represented separate production.</p>
        <HistorySources ids={['S002','S003','S008']} />
      </section>
      <section id="change"><h2>There was no single global ending.</h2>
        <p>Davis, Gallman and Gleiter examine American whaling through changing product demand and the alternatives available to labor and capital. Rocha, Clapham and Ivashchenko document a different twentieth-century setting, with mechanized catching and industrial processing.</p>
        <p>Our synthesis: the replacement of a particular lighting fuel cannot, on its own, explain the history of every later whaling industry. Markets, whale populations, costs, technology, trade and regulation need to be examined together in each case.</p>
        <p>Business closure, a government’s policy and a binding legal prohibition are separate events. Australia’s government dates the final commercial station’s closure to 1978 and the national policy change to 1979. One date should not stand in for the other.</p>
        <HistorySources ids={['S006','S032','S016']} />
      </section>
      <section id="communities"><h2>People belong in the economic record.</h2>
        <p>The Canadian report documents seasonal work and closure compensation. Later local fish-processing employment does not establish that the same former whalers obtained equivalent earnings. That question requires worker and household evidence.</p>
        <p>Indigenous and subsistence whaling need their own account of food, culture and community life. Commercial sale of some products does not, by itself, settle the primary purpose of a hunt. The IWC treats Aboriginal Subsistence Whaling separately from commercial whaling.</p>
        <p><a href="https://iwc.int/management-and-conservation/whaling/aboriginal">Read the IWC’s subsistence framework</a> and our <a href="/countries/">countries and communities guide</a>.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="method"><h2>What this first edition establishes.</h2>
        <p>We begin with a documented enterprise or operating episode: who caught or processed whales, where it operated, and what connected it to a market. A vessel’s flag, a founder’s nationality or a port visit is insufficient on its own.</p>
        <p>Government records, scholarly analysis, institutional histories and archive descriptions have different evidentiary roles. The <a href="/countries/history/">inventory</a> identifies its sources and limits beside each entry. A catalogue of company ledgers is not a claim that those ledgers have been read.</p>
        <h3>Still unresolved</h3>
        <ul><li>A complete global inventory, including smaller enterprises and historical territorial attribution.</li><li>Comparable annual values, consistent accounting boundaries and any defensible global monetary total.</li><li>Many national final-catch dates, company closure causes and subsequent worker outcomes.</li><li>The archive’s provisional Icelandic 2026 catch figure, Japanese 2026 quota lead and discrepancies in Brazilian mass and meal-sales records. These are not used as accepted figures here.</li></ul>
        <p>Neither an omission nor an unresolved entry is evidence that a place had no whaling history. <a href="/contact/">Corrections and additional sources are welcome.</a></p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
